INT. KENA’S BEDROOM - CONTINUOUS A clean, minimalist apartment. An alarm flashes 6:00 AM. KENA WASHINGTON (29) rolls over, stares at the ceiling, and sighs.


KENA (V.O.) 6 o’clock a.m. Damn, time to get up already! Well, let me see what I got to do today. I have to get Denise a gift for her birthday, call Mom, and pick up some things from the store. Well, let me get up out this bed before I miss my train.


Kena turns toward the camera, breaking the fourth wall with an intimate, grounded smile.


KENA (TO CAMERA) Oh, excuse me. I didn’t even notice you there. My name is Kena. Kena Washington. I’ve been living here in Chicago all my life. My father left me, my mom, and brothers when I was 10. My mom did her best to raise us and get us most of the stuff we wanted until my oldest brother Marcus was killed. He was involved in a drug deal gone badly, and my mother was all to pieces—as well as my other brother Charles and I, but Mom took it the worst.


Kena walks to her mirror, pulling her hair back.


KENA (TO CAMERA / V.O.) Charles got strung out on drugs and thought he was a Casanova when he was on powder and ecstasy pills. So I knew I had to do something with my life to make my mom proud. I went to college, studied business, and happened to get a lucky break when I graduated at this new insurance company downtown in the John Hancock Center. I still can’t believe I accomplished this much with my attitude and all that’s been going on in my life. However, I guess it is true what they say: you can accomplish anything if you put your all into it.


Kena grabs her trench coat and bag from the entry table.


KENA (TO CAMERA) I’ve been working with this insurance company for the past three years, and now I’m the supervising manager of their claims department. My life is slowly coming together to where I want it to be. I mean, I would like to get a car and some new furniture, but I guess having a nice apartment and a good job is better than what some of these other females have out here. Some of them are running from man to man thinking he’s going to take care of them for the rest of their lives, and some of them act like they got to sell their body to get money.


Kena pauses at the door, her expression softening.


KENA (TO CAMERA) I’m glad that’s one thing Marcus taught me that has stuck with me: don’t depend on a man for shit, get your own money, and save it. I miss my brother at times. He always had good advice on almost any subject or situation. Well, I gotta long day ahead of me, so let me end this conversation with you right now so I can get dressed and catch this train. I’ll talk with you.


EXT./INT. CTA TRAIN - MORNING The train rattles over elevated tracks through the winter chill. Kena sits by the window, coffee in hand, watching condensation drip.


KENA (V.O.) As I sat on the train heading to work, I stared out the window thinking about what I should get Denise for her birthday while trying to ignore the commotion of the morning conversations.


Across the aisle, a sharply dressed man leans down to kiss his girlfriend softly on the cheek.


WELL-DRESSED COMMUTER Have a good day at work, sweetness. I’ll call you later.


Kena’s eyes linger on them before looking back out at the gray skyline.


KENA (V.O.) Now this sister must be one of the lucky ones to have a man like this. But then again, maybe not everyone was having trouble with men except me. The closest thing I had to a good man was Carlos, and when shit hit the fan, this nigga was lying and cheating on me the whole time we were in a relationship. I felt so bad after that I thought I was just going to give up on brothers. However, then Curtis came into my life and made me feel like I still had a shot at love. Then after a year, he just stopped calling and coming around. I called him a few times and left some messages, but after none of them were returned, I just said forget it. I'm not chasing any nigga. I'm better than that. Now it seems all the good men are dead, locked up, or have turned gay. I've already come to the conclusion that I'll be single for the rest of my life. But, hopefully God will change that someday.


INT. JOHN HANCOCK CENTER - OFFICE LOBBY & MAILROOM - DAY Kena steps out of the elevator holding her Starbucks cup. Down the hallway, JOHN—fresh cut, smelling like cologne, tailored shirt—stands leaned against the mailboxes, running game on MICHELLE.


JOHN (flashing a megawatt smile) How are you doing this morning, Kena?


KENA (unmoved, grabbing her files) I’m fine, John. Hey, Michelle.


MICHELLE (smirking at Kena) Hello.


John gives a smooth nod and walks off toward the elevators.


INT. KENA'S OFFICE - CONTINUOUS Kena sets her bag down at her executive desk. Michelle follows right behind her, shutting the door halfway.


MICHELLE Can you believe this man and his lame-ass lines? Keith and I got into an argument right after having sex this morning, and then I come in here to John trying it.


KENA (laughing, shaking her head) How do you get into an argument right after sex?


MICHELLE Don't even ask, girl. Are you going to the gym tonight after work?


KENA No, I don’t think I’m going to make it tonight. I have to go get my friend something for her birthday and pick up some things for the store. I’ll be back in there with you Monday.


MICHELLE Well, let me go get some of this work done. I'll see you—what you want for lunch?


KENA I don’t know, we’ll come up with something.


INT. OFFICE CAFETERIA - 12:30 PM (LUNCH) Kena and Michelle carry their trays (Kena with a salad, Michelle with a cheeseburger and fries) to a booth. In the far corner, John is already leaning in close to a new female employee.


KENA He never stops.


MICHELLE I should go bust up his spot.


KENA Girl, leave that alone, because he’s going to think you want him then. And then Keith going to be up here trying to kill both of y’all tails.


They burst out laughing.


KENA (CONT'D) I know someone has told her about him.


MICHELLE I don’t know, she works in finance, I think.


KENA Well, I hope someone has informed her of his goal.


MICHELLE I hope so.


INT. RETAIL BOUTIQUE (JEW-TOWN/MAXWELL ST AREA) - 5:45 PM Kena examines racks of winter apparel. Her eyes land on a display of stylish Baby Phat boots.


KENA (V.O.) I ran across these nice black Baby Phat boots. Denise and I wear the same size shoes, so I decided to get the high-cut boots for me and the quarter-cut ones for her. We’ve been best friends since middle school. We used to tell people we were cousins. We would let anything or anyone come between us—even though one time this guy did have us not speaking for a couple of days. Ever since then, we made sure that wouldn’t happen again.


INT. KENA’S LIVING ROOM - 7:00 PM (SATURDAY) Kena stands in front of the mirror checking her outfit: a cream-and-brown sweater, brown leather pants, cream-and-brown fur boots, hair slicked back into a ponytail. She grabs the gift bag and heads out.


INT. DOWNTOWN RESTAURANT & LOUNGE - 8:15 PM Upscale, split-level venue with tinted windows and soft mid-80s R&B playing.


HOSTESS (flat, bored expression) Good evening, will you be dining alone or with guests?


KENA I’m meeting with someone that’s already here. They’re upstairs.


The hostess gestures to the grand staircase. Kena ascends, passing framed photos on the brick walls.


UPSTAIRS LOUNGE: Balloons, a bar, pool tables, and a jukebox. DENISE stands up from a corner table, waving her glass.


DENISE (tipsy, yelling over music) Happy Birthday to me! Kena!


KENA (hugging her, handing the bag) Happy Birthday!


DENISE Derek’s got the whole tab covered, girl, order whatever you want!


The WAITRESS arrives.


KENA I'll take a Blue Motorcycle.


As the waitress walks away, Kena looks toward the bar. Three men stand talking, but the man in the center has his back against the bar. Dark-skinned, close fade, clean dark blue jeans, crisp blue-and-white button-up, fresh Timberlands, and heavy platinum jewelry catching the light.


He locks eyes directly with Kena.


DENISE (whispering over Kena’s shoulder) How long you two going to stare at each other before one of you speaks?


KENA You crazy, girl. You know I’m not about to go approach that nigga. You can clearly see that it ain’t about nothing, even though he is sexy as hell. But as soon as that nigga get a piece of ass, you know he gonna be out the door or running a bunch of games, and I ain’t got no time for that no more.


DENISE You never know, girl. Looks can be deceiving and he could be the one for you.


KENA You could be right, but I’ll never know unless he comes over.


Kena looks back toward the bar. The man and his crew are already walking down the stairs toward the exit.


KENA (CONT'D) Well, I guess he wasn’t too interested and wasn’t the one for me.


DENISE Oh, well… Me, Derek, and a couple other people are going to the club around the corner when we leave here. We’ll give you a ride home if you trying to go with us.


KENA (smiling, taking a sip of her drink) Let’s go.


